<h1>Linear Regression: OLS From First Principles</h1>
<p class="lead">Why this matters: this repo now uses ordinary least squares in three genuinely different roles — as a <strong>hedge-ratio estimator</strong> (<code>solutions/03_stat_arb_risk/pairs.py</code>, the stat-arb exercise you'll implement), as a <strong>hypothesis test</strong> (the Dickey-Fuller stat in the same file), and as an <strong>inference engine in silicon</strong> (<code>fpga/linear_regression_predictor.vhd</code>). One piece of math, three jobs. Lesson 0003 introduced the model; this lesson derives it.</p>

<h2>1. The problem and the solution</h2>
<p>Given n observations of a target y and p features (stacked into a matrix X with a leading column of 1s for the intercept), find the coefficient vector β that minimizes the sum of squared errors:</p>
<div class="callout"><p style="margin:0"><strong>min<sub>β</sub> ‖y − Xβ‖²</strong>. Setting the gradient to zero gives the <strong>normal equations</strong>: XᵀXβ = Xᵀy, hence <strong>β̂ = (XᵀX)⁻¹Xᵀy</strong>.<sup><a class="fn" href="#fn-1">[1]</a></sup></p></div>
<p>Why squared error? Three reasons stack: it makes the objective differentiable and convex (one global minimum, closed form); it equals the maximum-likelihood answer when noise is Gaussian; and it makes the residual vector <em>orthogonal</em> to every feature column — the fitted plane absorbs all linear structure, leaving residuals that no feature can explain linearly. That orthogonality is what stat-arb exploits: the pairs-trading spread is <em>defined</em> as an OLS residual.</p>

<h3>The one-feature special case (worth memorizing)</h3>
<div class="callout"><p style="margin:0">slope <strong>β₁ = cov(x, y) / var(x)</strong>, &nbsp; intercept <strong>β₀ = ȳ − β₁x̄</strong>.</p></div>
<p>You will meet this exact formula again in Lesson 0028, where x = market returns and β₁ gets the famous name <em>beta</em>.</p>

<h2>2. Worked example — by hand</h2>
<p>Three points: x = [1, 2, 3], y = [2, 3, 5].</p>
<ul>
<li>Means: x̄ = 2, ȳ = 10/3.</li>
<li>Σ(x−x̄)(y−ȳ) = (−1)(−4/3) + 0 + (1)(5/3) = <strong>3</strong>. &nbsp;Σ(x−x̄)² = <strong>2</strong>.</li>
<li>β₁ = 3/2 = <strong>1.5</strong>, &nbsp; β₀ = 10/3 − 1.5·2 = <strong>1/3</strong>.</li>
<li>Fitted: ŷ = [1.83, 3.33, 4.83]. Residuals: [+0.17, −0.33, +0.17] — note they sum to zero (intercept guarantees it) and correlate zero with x (slope guarantees it).</li>
</ul>
<h3>How good is the fit? R²</h3>
<p>R² = 1 − SSR/TSS, the fraction of y's variance the line explains. Here SSR = 1/6 ≈ 0.167 and TSS = Σ(y−ȳ)² = 42/9 ≈ 4.667, so <strong>R² ≈ 0.964</strong>. Calibration for trading: an R² of 0.96 happens in textbooks; predicting next-day returns, 0.01–0.05 out-of-sample is a good year. High R² on a return target is usually a leakage alarm, not a victory.</p>

<h3>How sure are we? Standard errors</h3>
<p>Each coefficient comes with a standard error from <strong>σ̂²(XᵀX)⁻¹</strong>, where σ̂² = SSR/(n−p) is the residual variance (degrees-of-freedom corrected). The ratio β̂/SE is a t-statistic — "how many standard errors from zero is this coefficient?" This single idea powers the Dickey-Fuller test below.</p>

<h2>3. In this project</h2>
<h3><code>solutions/03_stat_arb_risk/pairs.py:11–21</code> — the engine, 8 lines of numpy</h3>
<pre><span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_ols</span>(y: np.ndarray, X: np.ndarray) -&gt; tuple[np.ndarray, np.ndarray]:
    beta, *_ = np.linalg.<span class="fn">lstsq</span>(X, y, rcond=<span class="kw">None</span>)   <span class="com"># solve min ||y - Xb||² stably</span>
    resid = y - X @ beta
    dof = <span class="fn">len</span>(y) - X.shape[<span class="num">1</span>]                       <span class="com"># n - p degrees of freedom</span>
    sigma2 = <span class="fn">float</span>(resid @ resid) / dof              <span class="com"># residual variance σ̂²</span>
    cov = sigma2 * np.linalg.<span class="fn">inv</span>(X.T @ X)            <span class="com"># coefficient covariance σ̂²(XᵀX)⁻¹</span>
    <span class="kw">return</span> beta, np.<span class="fn">sqrt</span>(np.<span class="fn">diag</span>(cov))               <span class="com"># (coefficients, standard errors)</span>
</pre>
<p>Note what it does <em>not</em> do: it never forms <code>(XᵀX)⁻¹Xᵀy</code> directly. <code>lstsq</code> solves the least-squares problem via SVD, which stays accurate when feature columns are nearly collinear — exactly the case where explicitly inverting XᵀX amplifies rounding error. The normal equations are how you <em>understand</em> OLS; lstsq (or QR) is how you <em>compute</em> it.<sup><a class="fn" href="#fn-2">[2]</a></sup></p>

<h3>Same file — three callers, three jobs</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Caller</th><th>Regression of</th><th>What's consumed</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>hedge_ratio(y, x)</code></td><td>price series y on price series x (+ intercept)</td><td>the <strong>slope</strong> — how many shares of x hedge one of y</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>spread_series / zscore</code></td><td>(uses the slope from above)</td><td>the <strong>residual</strong> e = y − βx — the mean-reverting thing you trade</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>dickey_fuller_stat(e)</code></td><td>Δe on lagged e (+ intercept)</td><td>the <strong>t-statistic</strong> β̂/SE — strongly negative ⇒ spread mean-reverts (5% critical ≈ −2.86)</td></tr>
</table>
<p>That's the full anatomy of OLS output put to work: slope, residual, and standard error each carry a different trade-worthy fact.</p>

<h3><code>fpga/linear_regression_predictor.vhd</code> — inference is just four numbers</h3>
<pre><span class="kw">constant</span> W0   : signed(<span class="num">31</span> <span class="kw">downto</span> <span class="num">0</span>) := <span class="fn">to_signed</span>(  <span class="num">50</span>, <span class="num">32</span>);  <span class="com">-- intercept 0.05 (×1000)</span>
<span class="kw">constant</span> W_F0 : signed(<span class="num">31</span> <span class="kw">downto</span> <span class="num">0</span>) := <span class="fn">to_signed</span>( <span class="num">300</span>, <span class="num">32</span>);  <span class="com">-- weight 0.30</span>
<span class="kw">constant</span> W_F1 : signed(<span class="num">31</span> <span class="kw">downto</span> <span class="num">0</span>) := <span class="fn">to_signed</span>(<span class="num">-100</span>, <span class="num">32</span>);  <span class="com">-- weight -0.10</span>
<span class="kw">constant</span> W_F2 : signed(<span class="num">31</span> <span class="kw">downto</span> <span class="num">0</span>) := <span class="fn">to_signed</span>( <span class="num">200</span>, <span class="num">32</span>);  <span class="com">-- weight 0.20</span>
</pre>
<p>This is the payoff of the <em>parametric</em> property from Lesson 0003: training produces a fixed, tiny parameter set, so inference is three multiplies and three adds — each <code>*</code> maps to a DSP slice, the whole prediction completes in 2 pipeline stages. Compare: shipping the 100-tree GBM to hardware means evaluating 100 tree traversals (the hls4ml problem). A linear model is the only model family where "deploy to FPGA" is trivially cheap — that's <em>why</em> it's the one living in <code>fpga/</code> despite being the weakest predictor.</p>

<h2>4. Often confused with</h2>
<div class="grid2">
<div class="card"><h4>Correlation vs Regression slope</h4><p>Related but not interchangeable: β₁ = corr(x,y) · σ_y/σ_x. Correlation is symmetric and unitless in [−1,1]; the slope has units of y-per-x and changes if you swap x and y. A pair can be 0.95-correlated with a hedge ratio of 0.3 or 3.0 — for hedging you need the slope, not the correlation.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h4>Regressing y-on-x vs x-on-y</h4><p>Not inverses! Slope(y on x) = cov/var(x) but slope(x on y) = cov/var(y); their product is corr², not 1. In pairs trading the choice of which leg is "y" changes the hedge ratio and therefore the spread. Convention: regress the leg you'll trade against the leg you'll hedge with — and test both directions for cointegration.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h4>R² in-sample vs out-of-sample</h4><p>In-sample R² never decreases when you add features — even pure-noise features. Out-of-sample R² (compute SSR on data the fit never saw) is the honest number and can be negative (worse than predicting the mean). Trading models live and die on the out-of-sample version.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h4>lstsq vs normal equations</h4><p>Same minimizer, different numerics. Forming (XᵀX)⁻¹ squares the condition number of X — with collinear features (think ma_5 and ma_10, correlated ~0.99) precision loss is real. SVD/QR-based solvers (np.linalg.lstsq) avoid it. Write the formula in your notebook; call the solver in your code.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>5. Practice</h2>
<div class="question" data-question><p class="q">Q1. For x = [1,2,3], y = [2,3,5], we found β₁ = 1.5 and β₀ = 1/3. What must be true of the residuals, by construction?</p><ul class="options">
<li data-correct="false" data-why="Nothing forces residuals to be small — a terrible fit also satisfies the OLS optimality conditions. The structural guarantees are about sums and correlations.">They are each smaller than 0.1 in absolute value.</li>
<li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. The intercept's first-order condition forces Σresid = 0; the slope's forces Σ(x·resid) = 0, i.e. zero correlation with x. These hold for ANY data — they're algebraic consequences of minimizing squared error, not signs of a good fit. (Check: 0.17 − 0.33 + 0.17 = 0 ✓)">They sum to zero and are uncorrelated with x.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="Residuals being normally distributed is an ASSUMPTION used for inference (standard errors, t-tests), never a guarantee of the fitting procedure.">They are normally distributed.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="All positive residuals would mean the line sits below every point — the intercept would simply rise to re-center them around zero.">They are all positive.</li>
</ul><p class="feedback" hidden></p></div>

<div class="question" data-question><p class="q">Q2. Why does <code>_ols</code> in pairs.py call <code>np.linalg.lstsq</code> instead of computing <code>inv(X.T @ X) @ X.T @ y</code>?</p><ul class="options">
<li data-correct="false" data-why="Both produce the full coefficient vector; output shape is identical.">lstsq returns more coefficients.</li>
<li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. Explicitly inverting XᵀX squares X's condition number, amplifying floating-point error when columns are nearly collinear (common with overlapping financial features). lstsq solves the same minimization via SVD without forming the inverse. Note the code still uses inv(XᵀX) for the standard errors — there it's the covariance formula itself, and the result feeds a t-stat, not the coefficient solve.">Numerical stability — inverting XᵀX amplifies rounding error when features are nearly collinear; lstsq's SVD route doesn't.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="For small problems like these, speed differences are negligible; the choice is about accuracy.">lstsq is orders of magnitude faster.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="Both approaches give the unique global minimizer when X has full column rank — the MATH is the same; only the floating-point behavior differs.">The normal equations give a different (wrong) answer mathematically.</li>
</ul><p class="feedback" hidden></p></div>

<div class="question" data-question><p class="q">Q3. Stock A and stock B have return correlation 0.9. σ_A = 4%/day, σ_B = 1%/day. You're long 1 share-dollar of A; the hedge ratio from regressing A on B is…</p><ul class="options">
<li data-correct="false" data-why="0.9 is the correlation. The slope rescales it by the volatility ratio — and here the ratio is large.">≈ 0.9 — same as the correlation.</li>
<li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. β = corr · σ_A/σ_B = 0.9 × (4/1) = 3.6. You need 3.6 dollars of B to hedge each dollar of A because A moves 4× as much per unit of B's movement. This is exactly why correlation alone can't size a hedge — it's blind to scale.">≈ 3.6 — correlation times the volatility ratio σ_A/σ_B.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="0.225 is 0.9 × (1/4) — the slope of the REVERSE regression (B on A). Direction matters: cov/var(x) uses the variance of the right-hand-side variable.">≈ 0.225 — correlation times σ_B/σ_A.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="A unit hedge ratio would only be right if the two had equal volatility and perfect correlation.">Exactly 1.0 — hedges are share-for-share.</li>
</ul><p class="feedback" hidden></p></div>

<div class="question" data-question><p class="q">Q4. The Dickey-Fuller test in <code>pairs.py</code> regresses Δe on lagged e and reports β̂/SE. Why is the <em>standard error</em> essential — why not just check whether β̂ is negative?</p><ul class="options">
<li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. On a finite sample, even a pure random walk (true δ = 0) gives a slightly negative δ̂ most of the time — estimation noise plus a known downward bias. The t-statistic asks whether δ̂ is negative by MORE than noise could explain, and the unit-root null even changes the critical value (≈ −2.86 at 5%, not the usual −1.96). Sign alone would 'detect' mean reversion almost everywhere.">A random walk's estimated δ̂ comes out slightly negative by chance in most samples — only the size of δ̂ relative to its noise (the t-stat) separates real mean reversion from luck.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="The SE doesn't correct the coefficient; it quantifies its uncertainty so the coefficient can be judged against it.">The SE corrects the bias in β̂.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="Scaling for interpretability is a side effect; the substantive role is hypothesis testing against sampling noise.">Dividing by SE converts the units to percent.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="It's the opposite — the test is needed precisely because the sign is uninformative on its own.">It isn't essential; a negative β̂ already proves mean reversion.</li>
</ul><p class="feedback" hidden></p></div>

<div class="question" data-question><p class="q">Q5. Why is linear regression the model family in <code>fpga/</code>, when the GBM clearly predicts better?</p><ul class="options">
<li data-correct="false" data-why="Licensing has nothing to do with it; sklearn and trees are open source.">Tree models are proprietary and can't be shipped to hardware.</li>
<li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. A trained linear model IS its coefficient vector — here four constants. Inference is 3 multiplies + 3 adds, mapping to DSP slices with deterministic 2-cycle latency. A 100-tree GBM needs hundreds of comparisons and branchy traversals per inference — possible on FPGA (via hls4ml) but expensive in area and latency. The accuracy-vs-deployability tradeoff lands differently when the deployment target is silicon.">Parametric compactness: four fixed constants synthesize to a few DSP slices with deterministic latency, while 100 trees would cost orders of magnitude more area and cycles.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="Linear regression is usually the WEAKER predictor here — the point is it trades accuracy for deployability.">Linear regression is more accurate on this data.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="VHDL can express tree traversal fine; it's just costly. The constraint is area/latency economics, not language expressiveness.">VHDL cannot express if-else logic, which trees need.</li>
</ul><p class="feedback" hidden></p></div>

<h2>6. Scenarios</h2>
<details class="scenario"><summary>S1. You regress next-day returns on the repo's 7 features and get in-sample R² = 0.31. Celebrate or investigate?</summary>
<p>Investigate, with leakage as the prime suspect. Daily equity returns are ~95%+ noise; honest models live at R² of a few percent. A 0.31 means one of: (1) a feature secretly contains the future — e.g. a moving average computed with a window that includes tomorrow's close, or the target shifted the wrong direction (the <code>.shift(-1)</code> discipline from Lesson 0003); (2) you computed R² on training data with many features relative to rows (in-sample R² inflates mechanically); (3) the data includes duplicated or overlapping rows. The test: walk-forward out-of-sample R². If it collapses to ~0 (or negative), it was leakage or overfit. If it genuinely holds out-of-sample at 0.31 on daily returns — you've made one of the great discoveries in finance, which is to say: check again.</p></details>

<details class="scenario"><summary>S2. Your pairs spread y − 2.0x passed the DF test on 2023 data. In 2025 the hedge ratio re-estimates to 3.1 and the old spread trends. What's happening and what's the fix discipline?</summary>
<p>The relationship drifted — cointegration is a property of a sample period, not a law of nature. Corporate actions, index reweighting, or a business-model change can permanently shift the equilibrium ratio. Holding the stale β means your "spread" now contains 1.1x of unhedged market exposure, which trends instead of mean-reverting — you're directionally long without intending it. Discipline: (1) re-estimate β on a rolling window (the same non-stationarity answer as <code>walkforward_sim.py --rolling_retrain</code>); (2) keep re-running the DF test — if the t-stat decays toward −2.86 and beyond, retire the pair; (3) size by the spread's recent half-life (<code>half_life</code> in pairs.py): if the half-life balloons from 5 days to 50, mean reversion has effectively left even if the test still scrapes by.</p></details>

